    "id" : "HCP_HTTP_REQUEST_RESOURCES_NOT_FREED_FIELD",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Ensure HttpRequest Network Resources Are Released",
    "description" : "This rule detects when HttpRequest fields do not release their network resources, potentially causing the application to freeze.",
    "explanation" : "# [Unreleased HttpRequest network resources (field)](http://fb-contrib.sourceforge.net/bugdescriptions.html#HCP_HTTP_REQUEST_RESOURCES_NOT_FREED_FIELD)\n\nFindBugs has detected an `org.apache.http.HttpRequest` (e.g. `HttpGet`, `HttpPost`, etc)\n            that didn't release its associated resources.  Code like the following:   \n\n<pre>code&gt;\nprivate HttpGet httpGet;\n\npublic String requestInfo(URI u) {\n    this.httpGet = new HttpGet(u);\n    try(CloseableHttpResponse response = client.execute(httpGet);) {\n        return getResponseAsString(response);\n    }\n    catch (IOException e) {\n        e.printStackTrace();\n    }\n    return null;\n}\n</pre>\n\n\t\t\t\twill freeze after a few requests, usually with no indication as to why.  \n\n\t\t\t\tThe reason this code freezes is because `org.apache.http.HttpRequest`s need to explicitly release their connection\n\t\t\t\twith a call to either `reset()` or `releaseConnection()`.  The above example can be easily fixed:  \n\n    private HttpGet httpGet;\n    ...\n    public String requestInfo(URI u) {\n        this.httpGet = new HttpGet(u);\n        try(CloseableHttpResponse response = client.execute(httpGet);) {\n            return getResponseAsString(response);\n        }\n        catch (IOException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n        finally { this.httpGet.reset(); }\n        return null;\n    }",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "HCP_HTTP_REQUEST_RESOURCES_NOT_FREED_LOCAL",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Ensure HttpRequest network resources are released",
    "description" : "Detects local HttpRequest instances that do not release their network resources before garbage collection, which can cause the application to freeze.",
    "explanation" : "# [Unreleased HttpRequest network resources (local)](http://fb-contrib.sourceforge.net/bugdescriptions.html#HCP_HTTP_REQUEST_RESOURCES_NOT_FREED_LOCAL)\n\nFindBugs has detected an `org.apache.http.HttpRequest` (e.g. `HttpGet`, `HttpPost`, etc)\n            that didn't release its associated resources.  Code like the following:   \n\n    public String requestInfo(URI u) {\n        HttpGet httpGet = new HttpGet(u);\n        try(CloseableHttpResponse response = client.execute(httpGet);) {\n            return getResponseAsString(response);\n        }\n        catch (IOException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n        return null;\n    }\n\n\t\t\t\twill freeze after a few requests, usually with no indication as to why. \n\n\t\t\t\tThe reason this code freezes is because `org.apache.http.HttpRequest`s need to explicitly release their connection\n\t\t\t\twith a call to either `reset()` or `releaseConnection()`, **even if the request is a local**.\n\t\t\t\tThe garbage collector will not release these resources, leading to the frustrating freezing scenario described above.\n\nThe above example can be easily fixed:  \n\n    public String requestInfo(URI u) {\n        HttpGet httpGet = new HttpGet(u);\n        try(CloseableHttpResponse response = client.execute(httpGet);) {\n            return getResponseAsString(response);\n        }\n        catch (IOException e) {\n            e.printStackTrace();\n        }\n        finally { httpGet.reset(); }\n        return null;\n    }",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
